Q. which css framework yu have used and whats difference from normal css?
A. 1. normal css : 
	Advantages
		No additional dependency
		Simple and universally supported
		Full control over styling
		Good for small projects
	Disadvantages
		Large projects can become difficult to maintain
		Repeated styles
		Naming conflicts can occur
		No variables/functions/nesting like SCSS

	Use it for: small websites, simple applications, prototypes, or when you want minimal dependencies.
	
	2. SCSS : SCSS (Sassy CSS) is a CSS preprocessor. It extends CSS with features such as:
		Variables
		Nesting
		Mixins
		Functions
		@extend
		Better organization
	SCSS is then compiled into normal CSS that the browser understands.
	
	Advantages
		Excellent for large projects
		Variables and reusable mixins
		Nesting makes styles easier to organize
		Good for creating a design system
		Very suitable for Angular applications
	Disadvantages
		Requires compilation/build tooling
		Can become complicated if nesting is overused
		You still have to design your own class structure

	Use it for: large applications where you want custom CSS architecture and reusable styles.
	
	3. Bootstrap : Bootstrap is a CSS framework/component library that provides ready-made styles and components.
		we don't have to write css for common style, It provide it like button, form-input, text-color, colors etc
		
	Advantages
		Fast development
		Responsive design support
		Lots of ready-made components
		Easy for teams to understand
		Good documentation
	Disadvantages
		Websites can look similar if you don't customize it
		Can include CSS you don't actually need
		Heavy customization can become difficult
		Bootstrap-specific class names can make HTML verbose

	Use it for: admin panels, dashboards, business applications, CRUD applications, prototypes, and projects where rapid development is important.

	4. Tailwind CSS : Tailwind is a utility-first CSS framework.
	
	Advantages
		Very fast UI development
		Highly customizable
		Consistent design system
		Usually produces relatively small production CSS because unused utilities can be removed
		Excellent for modern frontend applications
	Disadvantages
		HTML can become class-heavy
		Initial learning curve
		Less convenient if your team strongly prefers traditional CSS
		Complex reusable components need some discipline

	Use it for: modern React/Angular/Vue applications, SaaS products, custom dashboards, design-system-driven applications, and projects requiring highly customized UI.

	Instead of creating your own CSS class:

	.button {
	  background: blue;
	  color: white;
	  padding: 10px;
	}

	you compose utility classes directly in HTML:

	<button class="bg-blue-500 text-white px-4 py-2 rounded">
	  Submit
	</button>

	Common utilities:

	flex
	grid
	p-4
	m-2
	text-center
	text-lg
	font-bold
	bg-blue-500
	rounded
	shadow

