Q. what is box model in css?
A. The CSS Box Model describes how every HTML element is represented as a rectangular box.
	It describes how the browser calculates the size and spacing of every HTML element
    It consists of 4 parts, from inside to outside:
	
	1. Content : The actual content of the element.
	2. Padding : Space between content and border.
	3. Border : The boundary around the padding/content.
	4. Margin : Space outside the border, separating the element from other elements.

Q. what are Pseudo classes?
A. A pseudo-class is a CSS keyword used to select an element based on its state, 
	position, or user interaction, without adding a special class to the HTML.

	Pseudo-classes start with a single colon :.

	button:hover {
	  background-color: blue;
	}

| Pseudo-class    | Meaning                            |
| --------------- | ---------------------------------- |
| `:hover`        | Mouse is over the element          |
| `:active`       | Element is being activated/clicked |
| `:focus`        | Element has focus                  |
| `:visited`      | Link has been visited              |
| `:first-child`  | Element is the first child         |
| `:last-child`   | Element is the last child          |
| `:nth-child(n)` | Selects a specific child           |
| `:not()`        | Excludes matching elements         |
| `:checked`      | Checkbox/radio is checked          |
| `:disabled`     | Form element is disabled           |
| `:enabled`      | Form element is enabled            |
| `:required`     | Required form element              |
| `:valid`        | Form value is valid                |
| `:invalid`      | Form value is invalid              |


Q. What is CSS Specificity?
A. CSS Specificity is the set of rules the browser uses to determine 
    which CSS rule should be applied when multiple rules target the same element.
    suppose : <p id="title" class="text">Hello World</p>
    p {
      color: blue;
    }    
    .text {
      color: green;
    }    
    #title {
      color: red;
    }
    here 3 color is present but id has higher Specificity, so color will be red.
    | Selector                                                             |                                  Specificity |
    | -------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------: |
    | Universal `*`                                                        |                                            0 |
    | Element (`p`, `div`, `h1`)                                           |                                            1 |
    | Class (`.box`), Attribute (`[type="text"]`), Pseudo-class (`:hover`) |                                           10 |
    | ID (`#header`)                                                       |                                          100 |
    | Inline style (`style=""`)                                            |                                         1000 |
    | `!important`                                                         | Overrides normal specificity (use sparingly) |

Q. what are different type of positions we have in css?
A. the position property controls how an element is positioned in the document.
	There are 5 main values:

| Position   | Normal flow? | Reference                   | Scroll behavior        |
| ---------- | ------------ | --------------------------- | ---------------------- |
| `static`   | ✅            | Normal flow                 | Normal                 |
| `relative` | ✅            | Its original position       | Normal                 |
| `absolute` | ❌            | Nearest positioned ancestor | Scrolls with page      |
| `fixed`    | ❌            | Viewport                    | Stays fixed            |
| `sticky`   | ✅*           | Scroll/container context    | Sticks after threshold |

	
	1. static — default : Every element is static by default.

	.box {
	  position: static;
	}

	The element follows the normal document flow.
	top, left, right, and bottom generally have no effect.

	2. relative : The element remains in the normal document flow, but you can move it relative to its original position.

	.box {
	  position: relative;
	  top: 20px;
	  left: 30px;
	}

	The original space occupied by the element is still preserved.

	Important use : relative is frequently used as the positioning reference for an absolutely positioned child.

	.parent {
	  position: relative;
	}

	.child {
	  position: absolute;
	  top: 0;
	  right: 0;
	}
	
	3. absolute : The element is removed from the normal document flow.
	It is positioned relative to its nearest positioned ancestor (relative, absolute, fixed, or sticky).

	.parent {
	  position: relative;
	}

	.child {
	  position: absolute;
	  top: 10px;
	  right: 10px;
	}

	Here .child is positioned relative to .parent.	
	If there is no suitable positioned ancestor, it is generally positioned relative to the initial containing block.

	Common use : Dropdowns, Badges, Icons inside inputs, Overlay elements
	
	4. fixed : The element is positioned relative to the viewport and normally stays in the same place when you scroll.

	.header {
	  position: fixed;
	  top: 0;
	  left: 0;
	  width: 100%;
	}

	Common examples: Fixed navbar, Floating action button, Chat button
	
	5. sticky : sticky behaves like relative positioning until a specified scroll threshold is reached, and then behaves like a fixed/stuck element within its containing scroll area.

	.header {
	  position: sticky;
	  top: 0;
	}

	For example, a table header can remain visible while scrolling.


Q. what is flexbox?
A. Flexbox (Flexible Box Layout) is a CSS layout system used to arrange elements in a row or column 
	and easily control their alignment, spacing, and size.
	It is especially useful for one-dimensional layouts — either horizontally or vertically.
	
	<div class="container">
	  <div>Box 1</div>
	  <div>Box 2</div>
	  <div>Box 3</div>
	</div>
	.container {
	  display: flex;
	}
	By Default:
┌──────────────────────────────┐
│ Box 1 │ Box 2 │ Box 3        │
└──────────────────────────────┘

	Important Flexbox properties : 
	
	1. display: flex - Makes an element a flex container.

	.container {
	  display: flex;
	}

	Its direct children become flex items.

	2. flex-direction : Controls the main direction.

	.container {
	  display: flex;
	  flex-direction: row;
	}

	Options:

	row            → → → 
	row-reverse    ← ← ←
	column         ↓
	column-reverse ↑

	3. justify-content : Controls alignment along the main axis.

	.container {
	  display: flex;
	  justify-content: center;
	}

	Common values: flex-start, center, flex-end, space-between, space-around, space-evenly

	Example:

	.container {
	  display: flex;
	  justify-content: space-between;
	}
	Box 1                 Box 2                 Box 3
	│-----------------------space-----------------------│
	
	4. align-items : Controls alignment along the cross axis.

	.container {
	  display: flex;
	  align-items: center;
	}

	Common values: flex-start, center, flex-end, stretch, baseline
	
	5. gap : Controls spacing between flex items.

	.container {
	  display: flex;
	  gap: 20px;
	}

	This is generally cleaner than adding margins to every child.

